<h2>5. Technology Stack and Why It Is Used</h2>
<table><tr><th>Technology</th><th>Use in this project</th><th>General reason for using it</th></tr>
<tr><td>Python</td><td>All application and inspection logic</td><td>Rapid development and a mature ecosystem for PDFs, image processing, GUIs, and reporting.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PyMuPDF</td><td>PDF pages, text, outlines, geometry, rendering, and drawing inspection</td><td>Fast, broad PDF API with good control over page coordinates and rendering.</td></tr>
<tr><td>OpenCV</td><td>Template matching, morphology, connected components, QR/barcode fallback, page diff</td><td>Efficient computer-vision primitives for visual comparison and shape analysis.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NumPy</td><td>Pixel masks, grayscale analysis, ink statistics, and numerical image operations</td><td>Fast vectorized array operations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pillow</td><td>Image conversion, resizing, previews, and composites</td><td>Simple, reliable Python image manipulation and GUI integration.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pyzbar/ZBar</td><td>Barcode and QR decoding</td><td>Reads actual encoded data where presence-only detection is insufficient.</td></tr>
<tr><td>pdfplumber</td><td>Secondary table detection and standalone utilities</td><td>Useful PDF text/table extraction complement to PyMuPDF.</td></tr>
<tr><td>OpenPyXL</td><td>Excel report creation and formatting</td><td>Creates native XLSX files without Excel automation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CustomTkinter</td><td>Modern Xylem-branded desktop controls and tabs</td><td>Provides a more modern appearance while preserving Tkinter’s desktop portability.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tkinter/ttk</td><td>Native canvases, dialogs, Treeviews, scrolling, and fallback UI</td><td>Built into Python and reliable for desktop widgets and large tables.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Threading/queues</td><td>Responsive GUI during scans and safe main-thread UI updates</td><td>Prevents long PDF operations from freezing Tkinter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ProcessPoolExecutor</td><td>Parallel per-page scanning for long documents</td><td>Uses multiple CPU cores for expensive PDF/image analysis.</td></tr>
<tr><td>JSON</td><td>Settings and reusable templates</td><td>Human-readable, portable configuration format.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PyInstaller</td><td>Standalone executable packaging</td><td>Allows distribution to users without requiring a Python installation.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>6. Running the Project</h2>
<pre>git clone https://github.com/giriharan007/SpotCheck--Xylem.git
cd SpotCheck--Xylem
pip install -r requirements.txt
python run_gui.py</pre>
<p>Command-line execution:</p>
<pre>python -m core.pipeline --english "Input\\English" --translated "Input\\Translated" --output "Output"
python -m core.barcode_qr --master "&lt;master.pdf&gt;" --target "Input\\Translated"
python -m gui.region_dialog</pre>
<p>Packaging:</p>
<pre>python check_imports.py
.\\build_exe.bat
./build_app.sh</pre>

<h2>7. Quality Verdict Rules</h2>
<ul><li>TOC passes only when numeric topic sequences match exactly.</li><li>Barcode/QR checks pass when master and translated counts match; structural-only detection is explicitly reported.</li><li>Image comparison uses an approximately 80% pass threshold and a separate credibility floor.</li><li>Symmetric image counts pass only when both documents contain the same counts at the selected granularity.</li><li>Region verdicts depend on the selected mode and configured thresholds.</li><li>Text collisions and untranslated lines lower the final report verdict.</li><li>The final master verdict is intended to be PASS only when all required active checks pass.</li></ul>

<h2>8. Engineering Observations</h2>
<div class="note"><b>Repository consistency:</b> The committed implementation is more extensive than some older README descriptions. The pipeline currently creates additional report sheets for metadata, stylesheet results, text overlap, and untranslated text. The README also references a test file that was not present in the observed top-level tree.</div>
<ul><li>The architecture cleanly separates headless inspection code from GUI code.</li><li>Document scanning is optimized through caching and process-level parallelism.</li><li>Visual comparison deliberately avoids comparing translated prose and language-specific code content as ordinary pixels.</li><li>The committed <code>settings.json</code> contains machine-specific Windows paths and is better treated as local configuration.</li><li>The <code>Tables/</code> scripts are auxiliary and not part of the main unified pipeline.</li><li>There is no obvious conventional automated test suite in the observed repository tree.</li></ul>

<h2>9. Conclusion</h2>
<p>SpotCheck is a specialized PDF localization and DTP quality platform rather than a simple PDF-diff script. Its main design principle is to compare the document properties that should remain invariant—structure, graphics, code presence, region placement, sheet geometry, and layout—while deliberately ignoring properties that are expected to change, such as translated prose, localized barcode data, QR URLs, and pagination caused by text reflow.</p>
